12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: F

The circumflex in French is an etymological / historical phonological marker, not a stylistic prestige marker.

The functions that are historically and synchronically attested for ^ in French are essentially all of the phonological ones:

* B – homophone / homograph disambiguation, e.g. sur / sûr, du / dû, mur / mûr, etc.;
* D – historical marking of vowel length / lengthening. In Old French ^ marked a long vowel, the length now being mostly lost but preserved orthographically;
* H – classic s-loss marking, the most productive source, e.g. forêt < forest, hôte < hospite, pâte < pasta, etc., a sibilant that was once present in both speech and spelling;
* G / I – marking of the monophthongisation / assimilation of former diphthongs and hiatus sequences to a single long vowel, e.g. Latin au > ô, ae > ê, etc.;
* A and C are the usual popular descriptions of the same lengthening / openness correlation, e.g. ê /ɛ/ vs é /e/, ô /o/ vs o /o/, â /a~ɑ/. The circumflex regularly marks the historically long / open reflex.

Making a word look more learned, archaic or prestigious is a sociolinguistic effect that may be post-hoc, but it is not an attested orthographic function of the French circumflex in the grammatical tradition.

