10.text:
F. To make a word appear more prestigious.

The circumflex is a historical-etymological marker in the French normative tradition. Its documented, codified uses are phonological / morpho-orthographic:

* B homophone / homograph disambiguation, du / dû, sur / sûr, mur / mûr, prêt / près, etc.;
* H classic s-loss / consonant-loss marking, the most productive source of the sign, forêt < forestem, hôte < hostem, pâte < pasta, fête < festa, etc., i.e. a sibilant or other post-vocalic consonant that once existed in both speech and spelling;
* D historical vowel length / compensatory lengthening. In Old / early Middle French the circumflex is the graphic reflex of a long vowel, length that arose from Latin long vowels and from compensatory lengthening after the loss described under H. The modern open quality of â, ê, ô is the phonetic reflex of that historical length;
* G / I monophthongisation / hiatus resolution. The same lengthening environment is described as the coalescence of an earlier diphthong or of a vowel hiatus into a single long vowel, e.g. Latin au > ô, ae > ê, âme < anima, âge < aetatem. The circumflex records the reduction.

A and C are modern descriptive re-readings of that length / loss system. The contrastive closed/open system of French is normatively carried by the aigu / grave pair é / è, and the circumflex is homophonous with a/â, e/ê, o/ô in modern French. ô is in fact the regular reflex of Latin long ō, but the circumflex is not a rule “write /o/ from Latin”, it is the outcome of the length/loss system and it occurs on a, e, i, o, u from Latin, Old French, Germanic etc.

A purely stylistic, prestige-enhancing graphic use is a sociolinguistic commentary on the conservatism of French spelling, not a normative orthographic function listed by the Académie or by the historical grammars. Retention or addition of ˆ for aesthetic or learned effect is folk perception, not an attested function of the diacritic.

